 "caption": "Same box. Less chocolate. Sales barely moved. Then people noticed.\n\nIn 2016 Toblerone made its UK bars lighter (400g to 360g, 170g to 150g) by widening the gaps between the peaks. The box stayed the same. The maker pointed to ingredient costs; people mocked the gaps online. In 2018 Mondelēz said the 150g bar was no longer made and had only ever been a short-term response to cost increases.\n\nHere's the paradox. Ten years of US store data (Janssen & Kasinger, Marketing Science, 2026) show shoppers react to size far less than to price: a 1% price rise cut sales about 1.2%, a 1% size cut roughly half that. On paper, shrinking works.\n\nBut in pre-registered experiments by Ioannis Evangelidis (ESADE; Marketing Science, 2024; over 7,000 participants), people judged a smaller pack less fair than an economically identical price rise (chocolate: 44% vs 31% called it unfair). The driver was the feeling of being deceived. When the change was clearly disclosed, the gap almost vanished (37% vs 35%). France (since 2024) and Austria (since April 2026) now make large shops flag shrunken products.\n\nMy take: if you must cut, cut out loud. Say what changed and why, or keep the product whole and change the price.\n\nThe limits: the fairness numbers come from online experiments, not real shelves, and the sales data is from US stores; your customers may react differently. Sources: Marketing Science, Esade Do Better, INFORMS, NBC News, ConfectioneryNews, Service-Public.fr, Schoenherr. Toblerone photo: 2016 news photo (owner upload, via Kathmandu Post/AFP coverage); the other objects are AI-generated.\n\nIf you had to shrink a product tomorrow, what would you tell your customers first? Write to me.\n\n— Behnood Kavian",
